menu "OhEzTouch"

choice OHEZ_BOARD
    prompt "Target board"
    default OHEZ_BOARD_ARDUITOUCH
    depends on !IDF_TARGET_LINUX
    help
        Which panel this firmware is built for. This replaces the four
        PlatformIO environments, which differed only in their -D flags.

        The pin assignments themselves are not here: they are a property of
        the board rather than something a user chooses, and keeping them in
        main/port/esp32/board_pins.h keeps them reviewable in one table
        instead of spread over three defaults files.

config OHEZ_BOARD_ARDUITOUCH
    bool "ArduiTouch 2.4\" (ILI9341, XPT2046)"

config OHEZ_BOARD_ARDUITOUCH28
    bool "ArduiTouch 2.8\" (ILI9341, XPT2046)"

config OHEZ_BOARD_LANBON
    bool "Lanbon L8 (ST7789, FT6X36)"

endchoice

config OHEZ_ARDUITOUCH_JTAG_PINS
    bool "ArduiTouch: move the pins an attached JTAG probe needs"
    depends on OHEZ_BOARD_ARDUITOUCH
    default n
    help
        The esp-prog JTAG probe takes GPIO 12-15, which collides with the
        touch chip select (14) and the backlight (15). This moves them to 26
        and 16, as the ArduiTouch_debug_JTAG PlatformIO environment did.

        It is a pin remap and nothing else: the board is still an ArduiTouch,
        which is why it is an option here rather than a fourth board.

config OHEZ_ICONS_BUILTIN
    bool "Answer widget icons from the set built into the firmware"
    default y
    help
        Look a widget icon up in the icon set compiled into the firmware
        before asking openHAB for it. A miss is still fetched over HTTP, so
        this changes where the common icons come from and nothing else.

        What it saves is a request per tile on every page load, and another
        whenever an item's state changes the icon it resolves to -- the icons
        are most of the traffic this panel makes and all of it is for a few
        hundred images that never change.

        The artwork is not in this repository (it is EPL-2.0, and this project
        is GPL-3.0), so the set only exists once tools/build_icon_set.py has
        been run; without it this option has nothing to find and costs nothing.
        Turning it off is how to measure what the set costs in flash against
        what it saves in requests.

choice OHEZ_BEEPER_ENGINE
    prompt "Beeper engine"
    default OHEZ_BEEPER_ENGINE_SEQ
    help
        What the seventeen themed sounds are made of.

        The panel has one piezo on one GPIO with one LEDC timer behind it,
        and the frequency is a property of the timer -- so there is exactly
        one tone available at any instant, and that one tone is the entire
        budget. The two answers below spend it differently. Neither is free,
        and the choice is a taste one rather than a correctness one.

        Compile-time rather than a setting, because each engine has its own
        note format and therefore its own tables: the three families'
        fifty-one sounds are written out twice, once for each, and a panel
        carries only the set it plays. Making it a runtime switch would put
        both sets in flash for a choice nobody changes twice.

        The Lanbon L8 has no buzzer, so on that board this decides nothing.

config OHEZ_BEEPER_ENGINE_SEQ
    bool "Expressive -- melodies, envelopes, sweeps, vibrato and tremolo"
    help
        One note at a time, with everything that would have gone on a second
        voice spent on the note instead.

        A sound is a sequence of any length. Each note has an attack, a
        decay, a sustain and a release given as proportions of its own
        duration with a millisecond cap, so one envelope row describes a
        twelve-millisecond tick and a four-hundred-millisecond boot note and
        both sound like the same instrument. A note can sweep -- linearly in
        hertz, or as a glide that walks the period, which is both closer to
        musical and closer to what a divider actually does. It can carry a
        vibrato or a tremolo. And it can repeat itself, which is how an alert
        cadence is written.

        It steps every five milliseconds, so a sounding tune is two hundred
        wakeups a second -- fewer than a chord costs on the other engine.

        A chord is spelled as an arpeggio here, and that is a real loss: the
        LCARS blips are stacked fourths and fifths and this cannot stack
        them. Against it, an interleaved chord on this hardware has a grain
        on it either way, and two notes nine milliseconds apart read as an
        interval to most ears. If they do not read that way to yours, the
        other engine is still here.

config OHEZ_BEEPER_ENGINE_MIXER
    bool "Polyphonic -- up to three voices interleaved on the one piezo"
    help
        Real chords, time-multiplexed: each voice holds the channel for two
        milliseconds in turn, fast enough that the ear fuses them. This is
        what the panel shipped with, and it is still the right answer if the
        stacked intervals are the point of the sound design.

        What it costs: a grain on every chord, because chopping a tone at
        250 Hz puts sidebands either side of it; a floor of about a kilohertz
        under anything stacked, since a two-millisecond slot below that is
        under two cycles and the ear hears the slot rate instead of the note;
        up to five hundred timer reconfigurations a second from a task that
        runs above the renderer; and per-note expression limited to the three
        fixed envelope shapes it was written with -- no vibrato, no tremolo,
        and no beating between detuned voices, because phase is not carried
        across a slot boundary.

endchoice

endmenu

menu "Debug output"

config OHEZ_DEBUG
    bool "Boot banner and general debug output"
    default n
    help
        The banner naming the target, version, git hash and build time, and
        everything else that goes through debug_printf().

        Off by default, which is what every PlatformIO environment had -- so
        the banner has never actually been printed by a shipped build. There
        is an unconditional one-line version log either way.

config OHEZ_DEBUG_BACKLIGHT_CONTROL
    bool "backlight: brightness changes and the dim timeout"
    default n

config OHEZ_DEBUG_BEEPER_CONTROL
    bool "beeper: every note queued, and the task's stack headroom"
    default n

config OHEZ_DEBUG_BLE
    bool "BLE: every advertisement heard, and each scan window"
    default n
    help
        One line per advertisement, which in a populated room is a few
        hundred a second. Useful for finding out what a particular tag
        actually broadcasts; not something to leave on.

config OHEZ_DEBUG_CONFIG_FILE
    bool "config: the file name, and every setting as it is loaded"
    default n

config OHEZ_DEBUG_LED
    bool "LEDs: every brightness command from the broker"
    default n

config OHEZ_DEBUG_MQTT
    bool "MQTT: every topic published, with its value"
    default n

config OHEZ_DEBUG_OPENHAB_CONNECTOR
    bool "openHAB client: every URL requested, and the pages returned"
    default n

config OHEZ_DEBUG_OPENHAB_CLIENT
    bool "openHAB client task: every request submitted, dropped, and its stack"
    default n

config OHEZ_DEBUG_RELAY
    bool "relays: every switch command from the broker"
    default n

config OHEZ_DEBUG_SENSOR_BME280
    bool "BME280: each reading before it is published"
    default n

config OHEZ_DEBUG_SENSOR_MAIN
    bool "sensors: the refresh timer firing"
    default n

config OHEZ_DEBUG_OPENHAB_UI
    bool "openHAB UI: widget events, theme changes and the poll statistics"
    default n

config OHEZ_DEBUG_UI_MESSAGEBOX
    bool "message box: creation and timeout"
    default n

config OHEZ_DEBUG_UI_SCREEN
    bool "screens: pushes, pops and their transitions"
    default n

config OHEZ_DEBUG_UI_SETTINGS
    bool "settings screen: which tab opened, and what a save changed"
    default n

config OHEZ_DEBUG_UI_STYLE
    bool "styles: which theme variant was applied"
    default n

config OHEZ_DEBUG_WEBUI
    bool "web interface: saves, and the settings-table sanity check"
    default n

config OHEZ_DEBUG_WLAN
    bool "WLAN: the state machine, the access point and credential imports"
    default n

config OHEZ_DEBUG_WLAN_STATES
    bool "WLAN: only the state transitions, which is the quiet subset"
    default n

endmenu
